{
  "$comment": "NOTATION FOLD -- the seam between `notation_norm.fold_notation` (Python) and `notation_norm.fold_notation_sql` (SQL). Both are scored against the `expected` recorded here, and against each other. Recording the expectation rather than just comparing the two engines is deliberate: engine-vs-engine alone stays green when BOTH drift the same wrong way, which is precisely what a shared constant makes easy to do.",
  "version": 1,
  "seam": {
    "python": "server/notation_norm.py :: fold_notation",
    "sql": "server/notation_norm.py :: fold_notation_sql / fold_sql_text",
    "why_it_exists": "The fold decides whether two lot spellings are the same lot. It runs in Python (the reference, and any Python-side comparand) and in SQL (what a virtual join and a functional index actually evaluate). Two spellings of 'normalize' is the defect class this repository keeps paying for -- it is why the fold was layered on `canonical_key_value` instead of written beside it. This file is the layer below that, held to the same rule.",
    "what_a_disagreement_costs": "A join whose ON clause folds differently from the functional UNIQUE index that approved it: PostgreSQL stops using the index (sequential scan on a 10-million-row table) AND the uniqueness the gate verified is no longer the uniqueness the query needs. Neither shows up as an error."
  },
  "rule_combinations": {
    "none": "no rule enabled -- the fold must be the identity function",
    "sep_only": "separator run -> single '-'",
    "case_only": "ASCII a-z -> A-Z",
    "both": "the shipped default"
  },
  "server_symbols": {
    "fold_python": "notation_norm.fold_notation",
    "fold_sql": "notation_norm.fold_notation_sql",
    "fold_sql_text": "notation_norm.fold_sql_text",
    "separator_codepoints": "notation_norm.SEPARATOR_CODEPOINTS",
    "separator_pattern": "notation_norm.SEPARATOR_PATTERN",
    "case_alphabets": "notation_norm.CASE_SOURCE_ALPHABET / CASE_TARGET_ALPHABET"
  },
  "measurements": {
    "$comment": "Taken 2026-08-04 against the live database, READ ONLY, scalar expressions only. These are the facts that shaped the implementation; each has a corpus case that goes red if it stops being true.",
    "server": "PostgreSQL 18.3 on x86_64-windows",
    "database_ctype": "Korean_Korea.949 (datlocprovider 'c')",
    "server_encoding": "UTF8",
    "python_backslash_s_codepoints": 29,
    "postgres_posix_space_codepoints": 26,
    "postgres_misses": [
      "U+001C",
      "U+001D",
      "U+001E",
      "U+001F"
    ],
    "postgres_adds": [
      "U+180E"
    ],
    "regexp_replace_without_g": {
      "input": "WF.A_B 01",
      "output": "WF-A_B 01",
      "$why": "the 'g' flag is not optional; without it only the first run folds"
    },
    "upper_vs_str_upper": {
      "strasse": {
        "python": "STRASSE",
        "postgres": "STRAsse-with-eszett-kept"
      },
      "dotless_i": {
        "python": "I",
        "postgres": "unchanged"
      },
      "ligature_fi": {
        "python": "FI",
        "postgres": "unchanged"
      },
      "sigma": {
        "python": "folds",
        "postgres": "folds"
      },
      "e_acute": {
        "python": "folds",
        "postgres": "unchanged (ctype-dependent)"
      }
    }
  },
  "declared_divergences": {
    "NON_STRING_INPUT": {
      "$why": "Python's `fold_notation` returns a non-string unchanged. There is no SQL counterpart to score, and deliberately so: `_validate_column` REFUSES a declaration on any column not declared 'string', so a non-text value can never reach the SQL fold. The Python pass-through exists for callers that hold a value of unknown type, and is scored by the server suite (`test_non_strings_pass_through_untouched`), not here.",
      "scored_in": "server/tests/test_notation_normalization.py"
    },
    "NULL": {
      "$why": "SQL NULL propagates through `regexp_replace` and `translate`, so a NULL column folds to NULL and a folded join key never matches on NULL -- identical to the unfolded `col = col` behaviour. Python's `None` is returned unchanged by the same statement. Scored below as its own test rather than as a corpus case, because a corpus case would have to carry a `null` input type that no other case uses."
    }
  },
